| 5 | what-happened | The Second Circuit held that Barbosa da Cunha may be an applicant for admission but is not seeking admission, because he is not requesting lawful entry after inspection and authorization; he was already living here. So the court held that the detention statute does not apply to someone in the United States after entering without inspection and admission who was not apprehended while entering the country or shortly thereafter. The Second Circuit affirmed the district court, which had required a bond hearing or release. Under the July 10 memorandum, an applicant for admission may not be released from custody except by parole. Parole is a separate power. The parole statute lets the government release a person "only on a case-by-case basis for urgent humanitarian reasons or significant public benefit". The July 10 memorandum says the only people eligible for release on bond are people already admitted. | ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: Petitioner may be an “applicant for admission,” but he is not “seeking admission.”; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: because he is not requesting lawful entry into the United States after inspection and authorization; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: Section 1225(b)(2)(A) does not apply to noncitizens, such as Petitioner, who are present in the United States after entering the country without inspection and admission; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: who were not apprehended while entering the country or shortly thereafter.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: we AFFIRM the district court’s grant of the writ of habeas corpus.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10: may not be released from DHS