      </div>

      <section id="what-happened">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The grant</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="1">On October 1, 2026, the Supreme Court agreed to decide whether the government must hold, while their removal cases are pending, people who are living in the United States and were never admitted. The order takes the case; it does not decide it. Two laws are in play. One, called here the detention statute, says that in the case of an applicant for admission, a person seeking admission "shall be detained" if an officer determines that person is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted, except for a crewman, a stowaway, or a person an earlier provision of the detention statute already covers, and except that a person arriving by land from a contiguous territory may be returned there instead. The other, the bond statute, says the government "may release" a person on bond.<Cite s="scotus-order-list-2026-10-01" passage="THURSDAY, OCTOBER 1, 2026" /><Cite s="scotus-order-list-2026-10-01" passage="RHONEY, ACTING DIR. V. BARBOSA DA CUNHA, RICARDO A." /><Cite s="scotus-order-list-2026-10-01" passage="The petitions for writs of certiorari are granted." /><Cite s="scotus-question-presented-2026-10-01" passage="Whether 8 U.S.C. 1225(b)(2)(A) mandates the detention pending removal proceedings of aliens who, like respondent, are present in the United States without having been admitted." /><Cite s="uscode-2024-1225" passage="in the case of an alien who is an applicant for admission, if the examining immigration officer determines that an alien seeking admission is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted, the alien shall be detained" /><Cite s="uscode-2024-1225" passage="who is a crewman" /><Cite s="uscode-2024-1225" passage="to whom paragraph (1) applies" /><Cite s="uscode-2024-1225" passage="who is a stowaway" /><Cite s="uscode-2024-1225" passage="arriving on land (whether or not at a designated port of arrival) from a foreign territory contiguous to the United States, the Attorney General may return the alien to that territory" /><Cite s="uscode-2024-1226" passage="may release the alien on" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">For about thirty years, by the Second Circuit's account, people who entered without inspection and were not admitted or paroled, and who were not apprehended while entering or shortly afterward or held on a criminal or terrorism ground, could ask for bond. On July 10, 2025, a memorandum from the commissioner of Customs and Border Protection said the detention statute covers them instead. On September 5, 2025, the Board of Immigration Appeals said immigration judges lack authority to grant them bond. On September 26, 2025, officers arrested Ricardo Aparecido Barbosa da Cunha while he was driving to work in Norwood, Massachusetts; an immigration judge denied him a bond hearing, a district court ordered a bond hearing or release within ten days, and an immigration judge released him on bond after finding no danger and no flight risk. On April 28, 2026, the Second Circuit ruled the other way from the Board in Barbosa da Cunha's case. On October 1 the Supreme Court agreed to decide the question.<Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="Executive Branch practice over thirty years" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="without inspection and admission" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="who were not apprehended while entering the country or shortly thereafter." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="detain noncitizens on certain criminal or terrorism-related grounds" /><Cite s="fr-detention-rule-1997-03-06" passage="aliens who are present without having been admitted or paroled" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="in July 2025, the government changed that long-settled practice" /><Cite s="cbp-detention-notice-2025-09-18" passage="on July 10, 2025" /><Cite s="cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10" passage="FROM: Rodney S. Scott" /><Cite s="cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10" passage="section 235 of the Immigration and Nationality Act (INA), rather than section 236, is the applicable immigration detention authority for all applicants for admission." /><Cite s="bia-yajure-hurtado-2025-09-05" passage="Immigration Judges lack authority to hear bond requests or to grant bond to aliens who are present in the United States without admission." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="Petitioner-Appellee Ricardo Aparecido Barbosa da Cunha" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="arrested Petitioner while he was driving to work in Norwood, Massachusetts" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="Petitioner requested a bond hearing, which an immigration judge denied based on a determination that he was subject to mandatory detention" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="The district court granted the petition and ordered the government to either provide a bond hearing or release Petitioner within ten days." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="presents no danger to persons or property or risk of flight, released him on bond." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="we AFFIRM the district court’s grant of the writ of habeas corpus." /><Cite s="scotus-order-list-2026-10-01" passage="The petitions for writs of certiorari are granted." /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The detention statute uses two descriptions of the person, an applicant for admission and an alien seeking admission. It tells an immigration officer to detain that person for a removal proceeding when the officer decides the person seeking admission is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted.<Cite s="uscode-2024-1225" passage="in the case of an alien who is an applicant for admission, if the examining immigration officer determines that an alien seeking admission is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted, the alien shall be detained" /></p>
        <QuoteCard speaker="Detention statute" when="2024 compilation" where="Inspection of applicants for admission" label="Government Publishing Office">
          <p>in the case of an alien who is an applicant for admission, if the examining immigration officer determines that an alien seeking admission is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted, the alien shall be detained<Cite s="uscode-2024-1225" passage="in the case of an alien who is an applicant for admission, if the examining immigration officer determines that an alien seeking admission is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted, the alien shall be detained" /></p>
        </QuoteCard>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="4">The Second Circuit held that Barbosa da Cunha may be an applicant for admission but is not seeking admission, because he is not requesting lawful entry after inspection and authorization; he was already living here. So the court held that the detention statute does not apply to someone in the United States after entering without inspection and admission who was not apprehended while entering the country or shortly thereafter. The Second Circuit affirmed the district court, which had required a bond hearing or release. Under the July 10 memorandum, an applicant for admission may not be released from custody except by parole. Parole is a separate power. The parole statute lets the government release a person "only on a case-by-case basis for urgent humanitarian reasons or significant public benefit". The July 10 memorandum says the only people eligible for release on bond are people already admitted.<Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage={`Petitioner may be an “applicant for admission,” but he is not “seeking admission.”`} /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="because he is not requesting lawful entry into the United States after inspection and authorization" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="Section 1225(b)(2)(A) does not apply to noncitizens, such as Petitioner, who are present in the United States after entering the country without inspection and admission" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="who were not apprehended while entering the country or shortly thereafter." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="we AFFIRM the district court’s grant of the writ of habeas corpus." /><Cite s="cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10" passage="may not be released from DHS custody except by INA § 212(d)(5) parole." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="only on a case-by-case basis for urgent humanitarian reasons or significant public benefit" /><Cite s="cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10" passage="The only aliens eligible for a custody determination and release on recognizance, bond, or conditional parole under INA § 236(a) are aliens admitted to the United States" /></p>
        <QuoteCard speaker="U.S. Court of Appeals for the Second Circuit" when="April 28, 2026" where="Opinion" label="Barbosa da Cunha">
          <p>Petitioner may be an “applicant for admission,” but he is not “seeking admission.”<Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage={`Petitioner may be an “applicant for admission,” but he is not “seeking admission.”`} /></p>
        </QuoteCard>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="2">Barbosa da Cunha entered around 2005, lived in the United States for more than twenty years, applied for asylum in 2016, and held a work permit. He has never been arrested for or charged with a crime. If the government wins, people in his situation cannot be released on bond: the Second Circuit wrote that if the detention statute applies, he "must be detained during the removal process with no opportunity for release on bond". By September 10 the question had reached every numbered federal appeals court. Nine, including the Second and the Fourth, are the courts the Fourth Circuit said had held that the bond statute applies to noncitizens inside the country, like the people who filed the cases, and that they must be given a bond hearing. The Second Circuit's own holding covers people who entered without inspection and were not apprehended while entering or shortly afterward. Two, the Fifth and the Eighth, held that the detention statute applies to everyone who has not been admitted.<Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="lived in the United States for more than twenty years after entering the country" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="applied for asylum in 2016 and was granted a valid work permit." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="has never been arrested for or charged with a crime." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="must be detained during the removal process with no opportunity for release on bond" /><Cite s="ca4-bond-2026-09-10" passage="Decided: September 10, 2026" /><Cite s="ca4-bond-2026-09-10" passage="The First, Second, Third, Sixth, Seventh, Ninth, Tenth, and Eleventh Circuits concluded that Section 1226 applies to noncitizens inside our country" /><Cite s="ca4-bond-2026-09-10" passage="like the Petitioners, and that they must thus be provided a bond hearing." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="who are present in the United States after entering the country without inspection and admission" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="who were not apprehended while entering the country or shortly thereafter." /><Cite s="ca4-bond-2026-09-10" passage="The Fifth and Eighth Circuits ruled, on the other hand, that Section 1225 applies to all noncitizens who have not been admitted to the United States" /><Cite s="ca4-bond-2026-09-10" passage="they must be detained without bond" /><Cite s="ca4-bond-2026-09-10" passage="Today we join the overwhelming majority of circuit courts of appeal that have considered this question" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The government's petition asked the Court to wait on this case. It said the Court should hold this petition pending another petition, Lopez-Campos, which it called a "better vehicle" for resolving whether holding those people is lawful, and then dispose of this petition as appropriate. The October 1 order granted this petition.<Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="The Court should hold the petition in this case pending the resolution of the petition in Lopez-Campos and then dispose of this petition as appropriate." /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="Lopez-Campos is a better vehicle than this case for resolving the lawfulness of applying Section 1225(b)(2)(A) to aliens who are present in the country without having been admitted." /><Cite s="scotus-order-list-2026-10-01" passage="The petitions for writs of certiorari are granted." /></p>
        <SourcedBlock source="scotus-order-list-2026-10-01" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The Court's order list for Thursday, October 1, 2026, grants the petitions for review in a group that includes Philip L. Rhoney, acting director, against Ricardo Aparecido Barbosa da Cunha. The petition names Rhoney as Acting Field Office Director of the Buffalo Field Office of Immigration and Customs Enforcement, substituted for his predecessor.<Cite s="scotus-order-list-2026-10-01" passage="THURSDAY, OCTOBER 1, 2026" /><Cite s="scotus-order-list-2026-10-01" passage="RHONEY, ACTING DIR. V. BARBOSA DA CUNHA, RICARDO A." /><Cite s="scotus-order-list-2026-10-01" passage="The petitions for writs of certiorari are granted." /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="Philip L. Rhoney, Acting Field Office Director of the Buffalo Field Office of U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement." /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="Acting Field Office Director Rhoney is automatically substituted for his predecessor." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="scotus-question-presented-2026-10-01" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The question the Court agreed to hear asks whether the detention statute requires holding, during removal proceedings, people who are in the United States and were not admitted.<Cite s="scotus-question-presented-2026-10-01" passage="present in the United States without having been admitted" /><Cite s="scotus-question-presented-2026-10-01" passage="CERT. GRANTED 10/1/2026" /></p>

